<body>
<div class="report-container">

  <div class="report-header">
    <h1>UK Retail Fuel Price Trend Analysis</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">February 2026 &mdash; Based on Government-Mandated Forecourt Price Reporting</div>
    <div class="subtitle" style="margin-top:8px;font-size:0.85rem;opacity:0.7;">Generated: {datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</div>
  </div>

  <div class="report-body">

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="toc">
      <strong>Contents</strong>
      <ol style="margin:8px 0 0 18px;">
        <li><a href="#overview">Dataset Overview &amp; Coverage Summary</a></li>
        <li><a href="#price-by-fuel-type">Price Distribution by Fuel Type</a></li>
        <li><a href="#price-spread">Price Spread &amp; Market Competitiveness</a></li>
        <li><a href="#update-freshness">Retailer Reporting Freshness Analysis</a></li>
        <li><a href="#station-coverage">Station Coverage &amp; Fuel Type Availability</a></li>
        <li><a href="#key-findings">Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    {kpi_cards}

    <!-- Section 1: Overview -->
    <h2 id="overview">1. Dataset Overview &amp; Coverage Summary</h2>
    <p>This analysis is based on UK forecourt fuel price data collected under the government's open reporting mandate. The dataset contains <strong>{fmt_num(total_records)}</strong> individual price observations from <strong>{fmt_num(unique_stations)}</strong> unique forecourt stations.</p>

    <table>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Total Records</td><td>{fmt_num(total_records)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unique Stations (node_ids)</td><td>{fmt_num(unique_stations)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fuel Types</td><td>{', '.join(fuel_types)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data Range (recorded_at)</td><td>{fmt_date(date_range_start)} &mdash; {fmt_date(date_range_end)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Missing Values</td><td>{'None' if missing_total == 0 else fmt_num(missing_total)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Duplicate Rows</td><td>{fmt_num(duplicate_rows)}</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>The dataset spans from <strong>{date_range_start.strftime('%d %B %Y')}</strong> to <strong>{date_range_end.strftime('%d %B %Y')}</strong>, covering approximately {(date_range_end - date_range_start).days} days of price reporting activity. {"No missing values were detected across any column." if missing_total == 0 else f"Missing values were detected and are noted above."}</p>

    <!-- Section 2: Price Distribution -->
    <h2 id="price-by-fuel-type">2. Price Distribution by Fuel Type</h2>
    <p>The distribution of retail fuel prices varies substantially across fuel types. The table below summarises key statistics for each fuel type observed in the dataset.</p>

    <table>
      <tr>
        <th>Fuel Type</th><th>Records</th><th>Mean (ppl)</th><th>Median (ppl)</th><th>Std Dev</th><th>Min (ppl)</th><th>Max (ppl)</th>
      </tr>
      {price_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      {img_tag(chart1_b64, 'Box plot of price distribution by fuel type')}
    </div>

    <h3>Price Density Comparison</h3>
    <p>The violin plot below shows the full price density for the four major fuel types, revealing where prices cluster and how heavy the tails are:</p>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      {img_tag(chart2_b64, 'Violin plot of price density by fuel type')}
    </div>

    <p><strong>{most_expensive}</strong> is the most expensive fuel type on average ({fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc[most_expensive, 'Mean (ppl)'])}), while <strong>{cheapest}</strong> is the least expensive ({fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc[cheapest, 'Mean (ppl)'])}). Standard deviation is notably higher for premium fuels, reflecting greater pricing dispersion.</p>

    <!-- Section 3: Price Spread -->
    <h2 id="price-spread">3. Price Spread &amp; Market Competitiveness</h2>
    <p>Price spread analysis is central to understanding whether the market is functioning competitively. A wide spread means consumers in different locations &mdash; or simply visiting different forecourts &mdash; face meaningfully different prices. The interquartile range (IQR) captures the typical price band most consumers encounter, while the full range (max &minus; min) highlights extreme variation.</p>

    <table>
      <tr>
        <th>Fuel Type</th><th>Min</th><th>Q1 (25th)</th><th>Q3 (75th)</th><th>Max</th><th>IQR</th><th>Range</th>
      </tr>
      {spread_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      {img_tag(chart3_b64, 'Price spread and IQR by fuel type')}
    </div>

    <p>For the most common fuel type (B7_STANDARD), the IQR of <strong>{fmt_ppl(spread_stats.loc['B7_STANDARD', 'iqr'])}</strong> means 50% of stations charge within this band. The full range of <strong>{fmt_ppl(spread_stats.loc['B7_STANDARD', 'price_range'])}</strong> indicates that a consumer filling a 55-litre tank could pay up to &pound;{spread_stats.loc['B7_STANDARD', 'price_range'] * 55 / 100:.0f} more depending on which forecourt they visit. For E10, the IQR of <strong>{fmt_ppl(spread_stats.loc['E10', 'iqr'])}</strong> and range of <strong>{fmt_ppl(spread_stats.loc['E10', 'price_range'])}</strong> present a similar picture.</p>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Policy Relevance:</strong> The significant price spreads within each fuel type suggest that market transparency alone is not compressing prices to a narrow band. Consumers who do not actively compare prices risk overpaying substantially, which strengthens the case for interventions that promote price awareness at the point of purchase.
    </div>

    <!-- Section 4: Update Freshness -->
    <h2 id="update-freshness">4. Retailer Reporting Freshness Analysis</h2>
    <p>Under the UK's open fuel price reporting scheme, the timeliness of price updates is critical to the scheme's value for consumers. We measure update lag as the difference between <code>recorded_at</code> (when the system captured the price) and <code>source_updated_at</code> (when the retailer last updated their price).</p>

    <table>
      <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Records</th><th>Median Lag</th><th>Mean Lag</th></tr>
      {lag_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      {img_tag(chart4_b64, 'Distribution of update lag by fuel type')}
    </div>

    <h3>Freshness Summary</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Category</th><th>Threshold</th><th>Records</th><th>Share</th></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Fresh</td><td>&lt; 1 hour</td>
        <td>{fmt_num(fresh_count)}</td>
        <td>{fmt_pct(fresh_pct)} <span class="badge badge-ok">Good</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Stale</td><td>&gt; 48 hours</td>
        <td>{fmt_num(stale_count)}</td>
        <td>{fmt_pct(stale_pct)} <span class="badge {"badge-warn" if stale_pct < 15 else "badge-bad"}">{"Concern" if stale_pct > 10 else "Acceptable"}</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Very Stale</td><td>&gt; 1 week</td>
        <td>{fmt_num(very_stale_count)}</td>
        <td>{fmt_pct(very_stale_pct)} <span class="badge {"badge-warn" if very_stale_pct < 5 else "badge-bad"}">{"Concern" if very_stale_pct > 3 else "Low"}</span></td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <p>The overall median lag of <strong>{fmt_hours(lag_overall_median)}</strong> indicates that most prices are captured shortly after retailer updates. However, <strong>{fmt_num(stale_count)}</strong> records ({fmt_pct(stale_pct)}) have lags exceeding 48 hours, and <strong>{fmt_num(very_stale_count)}</strong> ({fmt_pct(very_stale_pct)}) exceed one week. These stale records are concentrated across <strong>{fmt_num(stale_station_count)}</strong> stations, suggesting systematic under-reporting by a subset of retailers rather than occasional delays.</p>
    {"<p class='note'><strong>Data Note:</strong> A small number of records show negative update lag (source_updated_at is after recorded_at), which likely reflects clock skew between systems. These records were included in the analysis but do not materially affect the findings.</p>" if negative_lag_count > 0 else ""}

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Policy Relevance:</strong> Stale pricing data undermines consumer confidence in the transparency scheme. Regulators should consider imposing maximum update frequency requirements and flagging stations whose reported prices may no longer be current.
    </div>

    <!-- Section 5: Station Coverage -->
    <h2 id="station-coverage">5. Station Coverage &amp; Fuel Type Availability</h2>
    <p>Not all stations report prices for every fuel type. This section examines how many fuel types each station reports and which fuels have the broadest coverage.</p>

    <h3>Fuel Type Coverage Across Stations</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Stations Reporting</th><th>% of All Stations</th></tr>
      {coverage_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      {img_tag(chart6_b64, 'Station coverage by fuel type')}
    </div>

    <h3>Fuel Types per Station</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>No. of Fuel Types</th><th>Stations</th><th>% of All Stations</th></tr>
      {fps_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      {img_tag(chart5_b64, 'Distribution of fuel types per station')}
    </div>

    <p>Standard fuels (E10 and B7_STANDARD) have the broadest coverage, reported by the majority of stations. Premium and specialty fuels such as B7_PREMIUM and B10 are reported by far fewer stations, reflecting both lower market demand and potentially less rigorous compliance with reporting requirements for these grades.</p>

    <!-- Section 6: Key Findings -->
    <h2 id="key-findings">6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
    <ol class="findings-list">
      {findings_list}
    </ol>

    <div class="footer">
      <p>Data source: UK Government Fuel Finder scheme, compiled by fuelcosts.co.uk (CC BY 4.0).&nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp;Report generated programmatically — all statistics derived directly from the source dataset.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>
</body>
</html>"""

# ── Write output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
with open(OUTPUT_HTML, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print(f"Report written to {OUTPUT_HTML}")
print(f"  Records analysed: {total_records:,}")
print(f"  Unique stations:  {unique_stations:,}")
print(f"  Fuel types:       {', '.join(fuel_types)}")
print(f"  Date range:       {date_range_start.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')} to {date_range_end.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')}")
